000124700000001\n\n> <PUBCHEM_MMFF94_ENERGY>\n84.9851\n\n> <PUBCHEM_FEATURE_SELFOVERLAP>\n50.859\n\n> <PUBCHEM_SHAPE_FINGERPRINT>\n10595046 47 18341050722671752083\n10906281 52 17968957356818371082\n11315181 36 18410293631968287321\n12107183 9 17547011823526410803\n12166972 35 9295281742775171278\n12236239 1 18413108329404690562\n12516196 113 18113339699695468837\n12596602 18 18202003201158153544\n13631057 29 18340767156452689107\n14251764 18 18410575084663078916\n14394314 77 18337394842837538849\n1454969 45 18260551139012751372\n14849402 71 18338521958167631864\n15131766 46 14564531897791911750\n15183329 4 18260824895611704699\n15419008 47 15841548553009562883\n15461852 350 17346590873258720405\n15728490 51 18410855499279025598\n1577012 14 18341050727188232827\n17093844 174 15719111366747749597\n17857418 61 18408602543971230203\n18681886 176 17846211185079408640\n19377110 9 18187357702223978400\n19489759 90 17846497007428055961\n20511986 3 18335409145900414579\n21033648 29 15864067702435072141\n21065198 48 18341049622970278547\n21150785 3 18272086110482069446\n21315763 178 18187366497594320298\n22224240 67 18187363247184876514\n23081809 10 18341329998445917514\n23522609 53 18194147181904369768\n23559900 14 18269828874015449105\n24771293 8 18335143120486161364\n300161 21 18335139765245706259\n3009799 131 16515682238664662548\n328311 84 13110963136850050988\n34797466 226 17274829077116758824\n4073 2 18260838115584755162\n4325135 7 18333731321437399390\n4340502 62 15574435419315135930\n504579 68 18413107281575069701\n5758199 1 18334580153246602626\n59755656 520 12463566279621662741\n67856867 119 18338801220623852465\n\n> <PUBCHEM_SHAPE_MULTIPOLES>\n485.95\n21.21\n2.01\n1.14\n15.07\n0.51\n0.18\n-1.46\n1.8\n-1.59\n-0.31\n-1.05\n-0.14\n-2.29\n\n> <PUBCHEM_SHAPE_SELFOVERLAP>\n1035.269\n\n> <PUBCHEM_SHAPE_VOLUME>\n274.3\n\n> <PUBCHEM_COORDINATE_TYPE>\n2\n5\n10\n\n$$$$\n","conformer":{"source":"PubChem 3D conformer","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/4679#section=3D-Conformer"},"properties":{"weight":383.4,"xlogp":2.4,"hbd":1,"hba":9,"tpsa":106},"brandList":[{"name":"Pantoloc","url":"https://medikamente.basg.gv.at/documents/1-21015__DOTC_FACH_INFO.pdf"}],"product":{"label":"Pantoloc 40 mg film-coated tablets","url":"https://medikamente.basg.gv.at/documents/1-21015__DOTC_FACH_INFO.pdf","source":"BASG"},"groups":[{"ruleId":"benzimidazole","name":"Benzimidazole","atoms":[7,8,10,11,12,15,16,18,19],"ring":[11,7,10,12,8],"count":1,"note":{"caption":"Same activating ring as omeprazole, but harder to protonate here.","body":["As in other proton pump inhibitors, protonation of the benzimidazole N3 makes C2 open to attack by the pyridine nitrogen and starts the acid rearrangement. In pantoprazole the 5-difluoromethoxy group strongly hinders that protonation.","A 2006 review rated it the least reactive marketed drug of the class, which fits a 2013 ranking that puts it ahead of omeprazole, lansoprazole and rabeprazole for acid stability."],"sources":[{"citation":"Roche VF. The chemically elegant proton pump inhibitors. Am J Pharm Educ 2006;70(5):101","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1637016/","licence":"Free full text"},{"citation":"Shin JM, Kim N. Pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics of the proton pump inhibitors. J Neurogastroenterol Motil 2013;19(1):25-35","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3548122/","licence":"CC BY-NC 3.0"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}},{"ruleId":"pyridine","name":"Pyridine ring","atoms":[9,14,17,20,21,22],"ring":[9,14,17,20,21,22],"count":1,"note":{"caption":"Weakly basic nitrogen (pKa about 4) that concentrates the drug in acid.","body":["Like omeprazole, pantoprazole is a weak base with a pKa of about 4. Its pyridine nitrogen is protonated in the very acidic canaliculus of the parietal cell, which traps the drug beside the H⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","In its uncharged form, this nitrogen attacks the benzimidazole to begin activation."],"sources":[{"citation":"Shin JM, Kim N. Pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics of the proton pump inhibitors. J Neurogastroenterol Motil 2013;19(1):25-35","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3548122/","licence":"CC BY-NC 3.0"},{"citation":"Roche VF. The chemically elegant proton pump inhibitors. Am J Pharm Educ 2006;70(5):101","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1637016/","licence":"Free full text"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}},{"ruleId":"difluoromethoxy","name":"Difluoromethoxy","atoms":[1,2,4,24],"ring":[],"count":1,"note":{"caption":"Electron-withdrawing OCHF₂ slows acid activation and changes cysteine choice.","body":["Pantoprazole has a difluoromethoxy group where omeprazole has a methoxy, at position 5 of the benzimidazole. The two fluorines make it electron-withdrawing, which makes N3 harder to protonate and so slows the acid-driven rearrangement.","Pantoprazole is the most acid-stable in a published ranking of the class, and it bonds to Cys813 and Cys822 of the pump rather than favouring Cys813."],"sources":[{"citation":"Roche VF. The chemically elegant proton pump inhibitors. Am J Pharm Educ 2006;70(5):101","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1637016/","licence":"Free full text"},{"citation":"Shin JM, Kim N. Pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics of the proton pump inhibitors. J Neurogastroenterol Motil 2013;19(1):25-35","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3548122/","licence":"CC BY-NC 3.0"},{"citation":"ChEBI. pantoprazole (CHEBI:7915)","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:7915","licence":"CC BY 4.0"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}},{"ruleId":"sulfoxide","name":"Sulfoxide","atoms":[0,5,11,13],"ring":[],"count":1,"term":"prodrug","note":{"caption":"The latent warhead: acid converts it into a cysteine-reactive sulfenamide.","body":["The sulfinyl group linking the two rings is not reactive on its own. In acid the molecule rearranges, and the sulfur ends up in a cationic sulfenic acid or sulfenamide that forms disulfide bonds with cysteine side chains of the H⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","For pantoprazole these are Cys813 and Cys822; the bond to Cys822 gives the inhibited enzyme unusual properties."],"sources":[{"citation":"Shin JM, Kim N. Pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics of the proton pump inhibitors. J Neurogastroenterol Motil 2013;19(1):25-35","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3548122/","licence":"CC BY-NC 3.0"},{"citation":"Roche VF. The chemically elegant proton pump inhibitors. Am J Pharm Educ 2006;70(5):101","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1637016/","licence":"Free full text"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}},{"ruleId":"aryl_ether","name":"Aryl ether","atoms":[3,4,6,17,18,20,23,24,25],"ring":[],"count":3,"note":{"caption":"A 4-methoxy pushes electrons onto the pyridine nitrogen.","body":["The pyridine ring carries methoxy groups at positions 3 and 4. A methoxy at position 4 donates electrons by resonance to the ring nitrogen, which makes the uncharged nitrogen a better nucleophile for the first step of activation."],"sources":[{"citation":"Roche VF. The chemically elegant proton pump inhibitors. Am J Pharm Educ 2006;70(5):101","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1637016/","licence":"Free full text"},{"citation":"ChEBI. pantoprazole (CHEBI:7915)","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:7915","licence":"CC BY 4.0"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}}],"pubchemUrl":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/4679"}